Environments:-
--------------
Environment is a collection of variables that lets you use different values for different setups without changing every request manually.

The easiest way to understand it is with your REST API project.
If you hardcode the URL into every request, you'd have to change every request when moving between environments.

That's bad practice.
So,Environment sloves this by creating different Environment
Local Environment
Test Environment
Production Environment


| Collection          | Environment                                      |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Stores API requests | Stores variable values                           |
| GET Employees       | `base_url`                                       |
| POST Employee       | `token`                                          |
| PUT Employee        | `user_id`                                        |
| DELETE Employee     | `password`/other config values where appropriate |


"so we can create n no.of  Environment in a workplace   ?"
Yes. You can create multiple environments in a single Postman workspace.
So the basic relationship is:
        Workspace → Collections + Environments


"so we can create n no.of Environment in a collection?"
No — this is an important distinction.
Environments are not created inside a Collection. They are created at the Workspace level.

So:
✅ One Workspace → can have multiple Collections
✅ One Workspace → can have multiple Environments
❌ One Collection → does not contain multiple Environments

"""""""""   Collection = API requests
            Environment = variable values
            Workspace = container that organizes both    """""""""

Variabels:
-----------
A variable is simply a name that stores a value.
For example: base_url = http://localhost:8080
Here:    base_url → variable name
         http://localhost:8080 → variable value


POSTMAN Requects:
------------------
GET    /employees       → retrieve
POST   /employees       → create
PUT    /employees/101   → replace/update
PATCH  /employees/101   → partially update
DELETE /employees/101   → delete


URL Parameters:-
----------------
1)Path parameter:  /employees/101
Here 101 identifies the employee.

2)Query parameter: /employees?department=IT
Here you're filtering employees.



